plan-approved = The user pressed "Go ahead" and approved plan "{ $title }" version { $version }. Start now: work through the steps in order. When you start a step, mark it running with the plan tool's update; when it is finished, mark it done and fill in output if it produced something (a document, sheet, deck or key points). Do not ask the user step by step; make small adjustments yourself and record a one-line reason in note. Only use ask, with two or three options, when you would go beyond what was agreed (deadline, spending money, sending messages for the user) or cannot continue. When everything is done, tell the user the result in a sentence or two, plus anything they still need to do themselves.
plan-continue = Continue with the next step of the plan, same rules as before.
plan-resumed = The user resumed the paused plan; carry on from the first unfinished step.
plan-chosen = About the question you stopped on, the user chose: { $option }. Continue the plan accordingly.
plan-step-done = The user says step { $step } "{ $title }" is done; continue the plan.
plan-stuck = Stopped at step { $step }; tell Xiaoban how to continue
tool-plan-description = Manage this conversation's plan. When something has several stages (the user asks you to plan it, prepare a presentation, move house, get ready for a trip…), first use propose to draft a plan. Only include steps you can do yourself, each with a short title and a date; things only the user can do (showing up, paying, signing, rehearsing) do not belong in the plan — just mention them in your reply. Put what was agreed in bounds (deadline, budget, no messages sent for the user…). After drafting, say only a sentence or two and wait for the user to press "Go ahead"; if they ask for changes, propose again and fill in changes. While executing, use update for step status, outputs and adjustments; use ask only when going beyond bounds or stuck. The plan card disappears by itself once everything is done. Don't make plans for one- or two-step chores.
tool-plan-title = The plan's name, e.g. "Friday client presentation"
tool-plan-bounds = What was agreed, e.g. "Done by Friday 10:00; never send messages for the user"
tool-plan-changes = What changed from the previous version, in one sentence
tool-plan-step-title = What this step does, briefly
tool-plan-step-due = Date or time, e.g. "Wednesday", "11/6", "Thursday afternoon"
tool-plan-step-detail = Extra detail (optional)
tool-plan-step = Step number, starting from 1
tool-plan-note = An adjustment or remark about this step, in one sentence
tool-plan-output-label = The output's name only, without a "Doc"/"Sheet"/"Deck" prefix, e.g. "Q3 talk outline", "6 key points"
tool-plan-output-id = The id of the document, sheet or deck, if a tool returned one
tool-plan-question = What the user needs to decide, and why you stopped
